-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Motion-Appearance Ambiguity:** In dynamic scenes, a single video provides only one observation per time step, making geometry estimation severely under-constrained. Incorrect geometry leads to artifacts when deviating from the original camera trajectory [S2].
-*   **Haze-like Artifacts:** Predicted depth is a weighted sum of depth values along the ray, leading to haze-like visual artifacts at novel views. Addressed by the empty-space loss [S2].
-*   **Disocclusion Artifacts:** Unconstrained spaces hidden from input viewpoints are prone to artifacts when disoccluded due to viewpoint changes. Addressed by the static scene loss [S2].
-
 ## Operating characteristics
-
 *   **Time Complexity:** Training takes approximately 48 hours for a network with about 100 video frames [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources. [S2]
-*   **Strengths:** Handles dynamic scenes from a single moving camera; produces geometrically correct novel view synthesis when combined with depth and empty-space constraints [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on input depth maps; prone to artifacts in disoccluded regions if static loss is insufficient; computationally expensive training [S2].
-*   **When to use:** Free-viewpoint video synthesis from a single moving camera with RGB-D input. [S2]
-*   **When not to use:** Static scenes (use standard NeRF); scenes with no depth information; real-time rendering (requires offline training) [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Strengths:** Capable of rendering free-viewpoint videos from a single RGB-D video stream without explicit deformation fields [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   **Limitations:** The method suffers from motion-appearance ambiguity, leading to under-constrained geometry estimation [S2]. It is prone to haze-like artifacts due to the nature of weighted depth integration and disocclusion artifacts in hidden regions [S2].
+*   **When to use:** When RGB-D data is available and explicit scene flow or deformation fields are undesirable or difficult to estimate [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   **When not to use:** For scenes with significant disocclusions or when high-fidelity novel view synthesis outside the original camera trajectory is required without the auxiliary losses [S2].
 
 ## Background
-
-*   **Origin:** Proposed in "Space-time Neural Irradiance Fields for Free-Viewpoint Video" [S2].
-*   **History:** Builds on the original NeRF framework [S2].
-*   **Usage:** Free-viewpoint video synthesis [S2].
+Space-time Neural Irradiance Fields was introduced by Xian et al. in 2020 [S2]. It is cited as a method that simply uses time as an additional input to render freeviewpoint videos from RGBD data [S1, unofficial]. The method addresses the limitations of static NeRF in dynamic scenes by extending the implicit representation to 4D [S2].
 
